## 2. Scope, Sources and Method

**Assets (basis for identification)**

| Asset | Address | Basis |
|---|---|---|
| DC01 | 192.168.100.1 | SMB/Kerberos/DRSUAPI server in pcaps; `DC01.corpnet.local` in SMB negotiate |
| Attacker host | 192.168.100.20 | Source of all attack traffic in the four cases |
| VICTIM workstation `WIN-MT4VSSDMUEM` | 192.168.100.100 | Sysmon/Security logs and Wazuh agent name (CASE-004) |
| SENSOR (Wazuh manager, Suricata) | 192.168.100.10 | Agent traffic to port 1514 in pcap (assumed role) |

**Sources:** DC01 Security `.evtx`, VICTIM Sysmon `.evtx`, Wazuh `alerts.json`/`archives.json` and per-event subsets, Suricata `eve.json`/`fast.log`, pcaps, tool output files (`krb_hashes.txt`, `asrep_hashes.txt`, `secretsdump.txt`, `lsass.dmp`).

**Method**
- Events from all sources were aligned on UTC. Where a source used EET it was converted (UTC = EET − 2 h).
- In CASE-004 the VICTIM clock lagged the sensor by about 79 s from ~05:55 onward (matched on three independent event pairs). Normalised times are given on the sensor clock.
- The logs for CASE-001 to 003 are the same files (identical SHA-256) and contain earlier test runs from 06:41 UTC. Each case is therefore scoped to its own time window (below).
- Confidence: **High** (directly visible in logs), **Medium** (consistent correlation), **Low** (inference or unexplained).
- SHA-256 of every collected file was recomputed and compared with the sums files and acquisition records.

