## 3. CASE-2026-001: Kerberoasting

**Window:** 2026-10-25 07:35:22 to 07:35:58 UTC | **Technique:** T1558.003 | **Confidence:** High

**Summary.** From `192.168.100.20`, the account `j.melnyk` requested RC4-encrypted (0x17) service tickets for the SPN of `svc-backup` twice, 35 seconds apart.

| Time (UTC) | Source | Event |
|---|---|---|
| 07:35:22.773 | DC01 Security | 4624 NTLM type 3 logon, `j.melnyk`, from .20 |
| 07:35:22.790 | DC01 Security | 4768 TGT for `j.melnyk`, enc 0x17, PreAuthType 0 |
| 07:35:22.818 | PCAP | TGS-REQ for `svc-backup`; TGS-REP 2 ms later |
| 07:35:22.819 | DC01 Security | 4769 service ticket `svc-backup`, enc 0x17 |
| 07:35:22 | Suricata | sid 1000001 "AD Possible Kerberoasting - Multiple TGS-REQ" (plus generic ET/SURICATA Kerberos alerts) |
| 07:35:56.996 to 07:35:57.023 | DC01 Security | Repeat: 4624, 4768, 4769 for the same SPN |
| 07:35:57 | Suricata | sid 1000001 |
| 07:35:58.033 | Wazuh | Rule 100201 (level 10) "Possible Kerberoasting - RC4 TGS-REQ" |

**Findings**
- `svc-backup` has an SPN and is served with RC4, so its password is attackable offline. The collected `krb_hashes.txt` holds one TGS-REP hash. Whether it was cracked is not documented.
- `j.melnyk` already had valid credentials at 07:35:22 (NTLM logon). In the log order this happens before the AS-REP Roasting of CASE-002 (see section 7.1).
- The two 4768 events for `j.melnyk` show PreAuthType 0, which also matters for rule 100203 (CASE-002).

**Detection:** Suricata 1000001 and Wazuh 100201 both fired. Wazuh latency from the second 4769 was 1.0 s.
**Impact:** offline crackable credential for a service account. `svc-backup` hash later appears in the DCSync output (CASE-003).

