## 4. CASE-2026-002: AS-REP Roasting

**Window:** 2026-10-25 07:37:51 to 07:37:53 UTC | **Technique:** T1558.004 | **Confidence:** High

**Summary.** The attacker sent three AS-REQ messages without pre-authentication data within 25 ms for `svc-backup`, `Administrator` and `j.melnyk`. Only `j.melnyk` received an AS-REP, which contained material crackable offline.

| Time (UTC) | Source | Event |
|---|---|---|
| 2026-10-22 (tool log) | `valid_users.txt` | User enumeration against KDC 192.168.100.1:88: 3 of 7 names valid (`svc-backup`, `Administrator`, `j.melnyk`). Log timezone unknown |
| 07:37:51.619 to .644 | PCAP | 3 AS-REQ (msg type 10) from .20 |
| 07:37:51.643 | DC01 Security | 4768 for `j.melnyk`, PreAuthType 0, enc 0x17, success |
| 07:37:51.645 | PCAP | AS-REP (msg type 11) for `j.melnyk` |
| 07:37:51 | Suricata | Only generic alerts (ET GoldenPac, Kerberos weak encryption) |
| 07:37:52.848 | Wazuh | Rule 100203 (level 10) "Possible AS-REP Roasting" |

**Findings**
- `j.melnyk` has Kerberos pre-authentication disabled (inferred from PreAuthType 0 and a successful AS-REP). The hash in `asrep_hashes.txt` is `$krb5asrep$23$` (RC4). Cracking is not documented.
- Rule 100203 fired 13 times in the shared logs, starting at 06:41:12 together with rule 100201. Because the Kerberoasting runs also produce 4768 events with PreAuthType 0 for the same account (section 3), the rule likely cannot distinguish AS-REP Roasting from Kerberoasting activity (inference, alert-to-event matching not done for every run).
- There is no custom Suricata alert for this technique.

**Detection:** Wazuh only. Latency from 4768 was 1.2 s.
**Impact:** credential exposure for `j.melnyk`, enabling the authenticated Kerberoasting seen in CASE-001.

