## 7. Cross-Case Analysis

### 7.1 Attack path versus evidence order

The cases are numbered as a narrative (Kerberoasting, AS-REP, DCSync, LSASS), but the evidence shows separate runs of each technique, not one continuous intrusion. For example, `j.melnyk` authenticates in CASE-001 (07:35) before the AS-REP Roasting that exposes the same account (07:37). CASE-004 uses a local account and a different host. The report therefore treats the cases as independent exercises sharing one attacker host and toolset.

### 7.2 Detection coverage

| Technique | Wazuh custom | Suricata custom | Other |
|---|---|---|---|
| Kerberoasting | 100201 detected | 1000001 detected | Generic ET/SURICATA Kerberos alerts |
| AS-REP Roasting | 100203 detected (low specificity) | none | Generic alerts only |
| DCSync | 100204 detected (21/21) | none observed | NTLM info alerts only |
| LSASS dump | 100205 partial (4 alerts, 6 dumps) | none | ET WMIExec (8), Wazuh 92074; Sysmon ID 10 absent |

### 7.3 Time to detect (Wazuh)

| Case | Triggering event | Alert | Delay |
|---|---|---|---|
| 001 | 4769 at 07:35:57.023 | 07:35:58.033 | 1.0 s |
| 002 | 4768 at 07:37:51.643 | 07:37:52.848 | 1.2 s |
| 003 | first 4662 at 07:38:53.987 | 07:38:55.018 | 1.0 s |
| 004 | dump #3 at 05:44:49.413 | 05:44:50.357 | 0.9 s |

### 7.4 Credential exposure

| Account | Exposed through |
|---|---|
| `svc-backup` | TGS-REP hash (001), DCSync (003) |
| `j.melnyk` | AS-REP hash (002), DCSync (003) |
| `svc-monitor`, `Administrator`, `krbtgt`, `DC01$`, `Guest` | DCSync (003) |
| `Steve` (local, VICTIM) | Used by the attacker (004); source of the credential unknown |
| Accounts present in LSASS on VICTIM | Not determined (dump not analysed) |

### 7.5 Common indicators

`192.168.100.20` appears in all four cases. The Impacket toolset is indicated in CASE-003 (secretsdump output) and CASE-004 (wmiexec command pattern and Suricata signature). The tooling for CASE-001 and 002 cannot be identified from the evidence.

